    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "6b0f4f66bad4b940a34a51917173d8132ceb76c8",
            "parents": [
                "556e04b8f374a9eb8cf32bf0e36ac46c14873eba"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/11/78011/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Christopher Sides",
                "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                "username": "csides-hpe"
            },
            "createdOn": 1738789794,
            "author": {
                "name": "Chris Sides",
                "email": "Christopher.Sides@hpe.com",
                "username": ""
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "It's quite helpful if you prefix the subject with the affected component, e.g:\n\n```\nDeviceMgmt: add driver binding support for existing devices\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "Good point. I'll do that for my other (related) PRs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "What does this mean?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "In HPE's case, there's a GXP-I2C driver that scans each bus, adding sensor devices from outside userspace -- which guarantees write attempts to delete_device from userspace for affected devices will fail.\n\nI would need to confirmed specifics on any GXP-I2C driver behaviors, but at the end of the day, we've got devices added from outside userspace that add_device and delete_device sysfs calls won't work on as a result."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "I went ahead and changed up the phrasing a bit"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "I still think it needs explanation. What is doing the scanning?\n\nOr is this somehow devicetree-based? Why must the drivers be bound by userspace?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "You might actually be right about the 'somehow devicetree-based' thing. \n\nI was talking to someone today who pointed out that as part of HPE's greater efforts towards an eventual 'one image' release (one OpenBMC image for all HPE platforms, with platform-specific config files pulled out of ROM), there exists some DTBO files that HPE's internal-build recipes are pulling into builds for our platforms.\n\nTurns out that when decompiled, these files contain DT nodes about the exact temp sensor devices I'd been testing with, and created this fix to solve around.\n\nI'm currently in the process of doing some testing to confirm if removing those DTBO files in question would solve the \"unbound devices exist that hwmontempsensors can't interact with\" issue without this PR being needed.\n\n-------------\n\nWhat are your thoughts re: sensors called out in DTS? \n\nEven if I can work around my 'HwmonTempSensor skips handling for existing devices' problem by removing some DTS nodes... \n\nI think it still makes sense to consider merging this PR so that dbus daemons like HwmonTempSensors can 'pick things up' if sensors are added to a bus, but no driver is bound for host power reasons."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "Confirmed: removing the device-tree binary overlay (DTBO) files from our builds allows HwmonTempSensor to work 'out of the box' with the sensors in question.\n\nSo you were right -- it turns out we're seeing HwmonTempSensor run into issues with devices called out in both DTS and in entity-manager config -- but the problem only arises if the host isn't already powered on when BMC boots.\n\nIf the host is powered on, and its sensors are available as the BMC is booting, then sensors called out in both the DT and in the entity-manager config seem to work 'fine' -- as in Hwmon nodes will be picked up by HwmonTempSensor.\n\nThis works since the drivers are able to bind to powered sensors at boot, and so the Hwmon nodes exist by the time the daemon starts checking to see if a new_device call is needed to create a new Hwmon node not)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Okay, so there's a race condition that emerges as a hazard because you're describing the sensors in two places. I think you need to figure out what to do about the latter problem. That might resolve the hazard in the former."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "I hear you there, and agree.\n\nThe \"existing i2c devices in sysfs with no driver bound get ignored by HwmonTempSensor daemon\" issue HPE ran into can be solved by simply not defining these sensors in the device-tree. \n\nSince realizing the source of the issue we were seeing, I've started digging into/having conversations on the \"why was was it done that way initially, and can we just skip doing it in the future?\" side of things. \n\nHPE's specific issue aside-- \n\nAs far as I understand the current situation, HwmonTempSensor daemon will pickup sensors called out in device-tree + Entity-Manager config, but only as long as the any sensors dependent on host-power are already online when the BMC boots (so they get a driver bound).\n\nWhat are you're thoughts re: OBMC supporting sensors called out in DTS? I feel like support exists today, but is kind of broken as-is."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "To clarify- \n\nEven if HPE has a workaround for the underlying issue (\"simply don't call out sensors in DTS\"), I'm still willing to keep working and pushing on this PR if we think it makes sense to fix/improve support for sensors called out in DTS.\n\nI think it does, but I'm still learning the bigger picture stuff..."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "This has always been the fundamental conflict of EM - if sensors are a fixed part of the platform then arguably the devicetree should be the place they're described. However, if they come-and-go across the boot life-cycle, then they should not be described in the static devicetree, as they're not always present.\n\nDevicetree overlays are not supported by the upstream kernel and so are not supported by openbmc. Since you're using overlays in a fork, I think this patch can be set aside for now (for the purpose of upstream)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "This solves for situations where a device is added to I2C bus from outside userspace, but the device hasn't been bound to a driver yet."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi Christopher, do you mind looking over the following change?\n\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/75497"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "We've already taken an early exit following the test for the inverse condition above, so this is always true, right?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "This was mostly chosen by the CI tool rules.\n\nMy original setup was something closer to:\nif (deviceReady)\n.. //do nothing\nelse if (devicePresent)\n.. // bind driver\nelse if (!devicePresent)\n.. // add_device -- original behavior"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "What do you mean by \"mostly chosen by the CI tool rules?\n\nYour original code suffers the same observation and could have been written:\n\n```\nif (deviceReady) {\n    ...\n} else if (devicePresent) {\n    ...\n} else {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nNo need for the `(!devicePresent)` condition, as we already know its true by nature of not taking the `else if (devicePresent)` branch."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "The chain of blame is a little murky-- \n\nI mean I coded one thing, aiming to make it 'obvious' what's being done, rather than brief-- made some tweaks and tested, ran the CI tool locally, accepted the changes and pushed to gerrit-- then had a \"What the heck did I upload again?\" moment when looking over that if-else section.\n\nI either uploaded an older iteration of my if-else chain, or the CI scripts suggested a rewrite for it (I think this one) -- or some combination.\n\nEither way, I'll try explicitly setting your version of the if-else and see what happens tomorrow."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "...maybe it'd make sense to have DeviceMgmt keep track of paths for anything it successfully did a driver binding for, then only unbind on delete_device fail if it's on that list. \n\nI'll do some experimenting there -- assuming no one chimes in to say it's not needed in the meantime..."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "Whoops - this last comment was intended for the child PR change @\n\n78014: DeviceMgmt: unbind drivers on i2cdevice::destroy | https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/78014"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 198,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "I have an update for this section here-- to check if a driver needs to be unbound on destroy() for devices added from outside userspace (delete_device writes will always fail on those).\n\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/78014\n\nIt's a separate fix because I didn't think about it until I was already uploading this change to Gerrit. It's uploaded for any comments on methodology, but will be marked WIP until I've tested it on a live system."
                }
            ],
